TwinShield

Network Digital Twin for Attack Forecasting

Overview

TwinShield is a cybersecurity research project that aims to create a Digital Twin of a computer network and forecast future attack risk before attacks occur.

Unlike traditional Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) that detect attacks after they happen, TwinShield continuously models network behavior and predicts attack likelihood for future time windows.



Problem Statement

Current IDS solutions are reactive.

They analyze traffic and detect attacks after malicious activity has already occurred.

TwinShield introduces a proactive approach by building a virtual representation of the network and forecasting attack risk using machine learning and network-state modeling.



Objectives

* Build a Digital Twin representation of a network.
* Model network behavior over time.
* Generate network state snapshots.
* Forecast attack probability for future time intervals.
* Visualize attack risk across network nodes.
* Develop a proactive cyber-defense framework.



Dataset

UNSW-NB15

The dataset contains normal and malicious network traffic records and will be used to model network states and attack behavior.



Planned Architecture

Traffic Logs
↓
Feature Engineering
↓
